<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Novempopulania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Novempopulana, Aquitania Tertia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Novempopulana was a late Roman province in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, formed around 300 from the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a>. Its name, 'nine peoples', referred to its Aquitanian tribes, though it later contained twelve civitates. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elusa">Elusa</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eauze">Eauze</a>, and the province formed the basis of the later duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/novempopulania.jpg" alt="Novempopulania" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image has been made by Zorion (Zorion) and released under the licenses stated above. … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Novempopulania1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The land that became Novempopulania lay in the triangle formed by the River Garonne, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a>. Julius Caesar, in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commentarii-de-bello-gallico">Commentarii de Bello Gallico</a>, called it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitani</a> who lived there, and described them as different in language and body type from their northern neighbours and closer to the Celtiberians. Augustus later enlarged the province of Aquitania to cover a much larger and more diverse territory, so that the old name no longer meant the land of the Aquitani alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between the 2nd and 4th centuries the nine peoples of the original territory seem to have won from the emperor the right to separate from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> proper, through the magister pagi Verus; an altar dedicated to the deity of the pagus, found at Hasparren, commemorates it. The new status may have affected taxes and conscription, since separate Cohortes Aquitanorum were raised for the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitanians</a>. The peoples later numbered twelve, each with its civitas: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auch">Auch</a>, Dax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lectoure">Lectoure</a>, Comminges, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/couserans">Couserans</a>, Buch and Born, Béarn or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lescar">Lescar</a>, Aire-sur-l'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Bazas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbes">Tarbes</a>, Oloron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eauze">Eauze</a>. Inscriptions across the province name deities, persons and places resembling present-day Basque, a basis of the theory that Aquitanian was a form of proto-Basque.</p><p>In 418 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> allotted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> as foederati. They settled along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, their seat, but their hold on Novempopulania may have been more nominal than real, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a> in 507 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> drove them out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-antiquity">Late antiquity</a> brought unrest: documents mention bagaudae and later raids by the Vascones, a name that by then had widened to cover all the Basque-language tribes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Novempopulania became the core of the Duchy of Vasconia, set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> early in the 7th century to hold back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, which later split into the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> and the County of Vasconia. Traces of Basque survive in the Gascon language, especially in its Béarnese dialect.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Novempopulania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their town was refounded as Augusta Auscorum, and in the early Middle Ages it replaced Eauze as the metropolitan see of <b>Novempopulana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ausci">Ausci</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome their chief town was Turba, later Tarbes, within the province of Aquitania and then <b>Novempopulana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bigerriones">Bigerriones</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 602 the Frankish kings created a Duchy of Vasconia under a duke named Genial to control the Basques, who had pushed north of the Pyrenees into the old Roman <b>Novempopulana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-gascony">Duchy of Gascony</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rome Elusa grew into a prosperous town, and in the late 3rd century it became the capital of the province of <b>Novempopulana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/elusates">Elusates</a></li></ul></section>
